1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,379
In 1848, an iron rod went through Phineas Gage's head.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:06,534
He lived, but he was never the same man.

3
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:11,262
September 13, 1848. A railroad crew in Vermont.

4
00:00:11,367 --> 00:00:15,056
Gage, a foreman, is packing explosive powder with an iron tamping rod.

5
00:00:15,167 --> 00:00:16,540
A spark sets the powder off.

6
00:00:16,667 --> 00:00:20,465
The rod, over three feet long, shoots through his left cheek.

7
00:00:20,567 --> 00:00:23,361
It exits through the top of his skull, and lands yards away.

8
00:00:23,467 --> 00:00:26,497
He is conscious, sitting up, and talking soon after.

9
00:00:26,600 --> 00:00:29,894
Doctor John Harlow cleans the wound. Gage survives.

10
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:33,763
But the man who recovers is impatient, rude, and unable to keep plans.

11
00:00:33,867 --> 00:00:35,503
Doctors study him for years.

12
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:38,831
His case becomes an early clue that personality depends on the brain.

13
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:41,166
He later drives stagecoaches in Chile.

14
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:45,315
He dies in 1860, twelve years after the accident.

15
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:50,737
One rod. One man. A new idea about the mind.
